In article <Pine.SV4.4.10.9905261448040.12197-100000@X.coe.neu.edu>,
"Anthony J. Gabrielson" <agabriel@coe.neu.edu> wrote:

> If you want to run intel software - use a fast intel.  Intel for a current
> box is far less.  I just built a dual PII 350 for under $1000.  It is very
> fast.  Alpha is just a cooler arch in my opinion.

I think it may be more than that; I have some heavy-duty numerical
software which I ran for several days on a 400-MHz Pentium II RedHat Linux
box and on a dual 500MHz 21264 Alpha box using Compaq's True64 UNIX with
SMP extensions.  While the dual Alpha is doing SMP, I didn't use any of
the compiler switches to parallelize the computation, so I assume it ran
on only one processor (at a time).

The Alpha was 4-5 times faster.

To be sure, the Compaq C compiler is better than gcc, but by THAT much? 
This is just one FAST machine.

Yes, use a fast intel to run intel software; but if you have source and
therefore the luxury of choosing which platform to compile on, I think the
Alpha wins hands-down.

--Ron Bruck



