Lance Reed wrote:
> 
> I own a AlphaPC 164SX.
> Last night was swapping ethernet cards (PCI)
> in and out.  I was trying to find out if the Linksys
> card I was using was my network problem or
> if it was a driver problem (My Linksys 10/100
> appears to drop packets?).  Well, I tried plugging in
> a.  3COM 3C905-TX.  When I did this, the machine
> on startup (before anything comes on the screen) started
> beeping at me.  1 long beep and three short beeps.
> I moved it to a 64 bit PCI slot, same thing.  SO, thinking
> it was the card, I put the Linksys card back in.  On startup it
> does the same sequence of  beep, 1 long, three short.
> It  continues to boot, counts memory, loads MILO, does
> the beep sequence again (1-3), and loads Linux.  Everything
> SEEMS fine.  But this is something new, and I doubt it is
> nothing.  My manual says a 1-2-3 (1 beep, 2 beeps, 3 beeps)
> is a fail-safe boot. I re-installed the Flash. not the problem.
> It says 4 beeps means, "No valid header found in ROM; loading entire
> ROM".
> 
> 
> So, any ideas out there if this is a problem that may leads to other
> problems and one day be the death of my board?
> Any help would be greatly appreciated.

If the failsafe booter is being used, the switch on the motherboard has
been changed.  Section 3-2 of the 164sx manual shows you which dip
switches are for what.  Try changing CF7 to 1 instead of 0 and it should
use the AlphaBIOS.

