On Fri, 28 May 1999 21:52:34 -0700, Ronald Bruck <bruck@pacificnet.net> wrote:

>Yes, use a fast intel to run intel software; but if you have source and
>therefore the luxury of choosing which platform to compile on, I think the
>Alpha wins hands-down.

But what is the price divided by power (need better word)?  Eg, if the
Intel costs $1,000 but has 1 units of power, and the Alpha costs $4,000
and has 1000 units of power (meaning that it is 1000 times faster),
then the Alpha is surely the better choice: its ratio is 4, whereas
the Intel's ratio is 1000.  This power numbers are exagerrated, just to
illustrate my point.  (Smaller ratio is better.)

Earlier in the email, you said that the Alpha was 4 times faster.  The
Alpha costs $4,000.  The Intel's power is 1, and it costs $1,000.  So
both computers have a ratio of 1000, and neither is better than the
other.

You also said that your test was floating point intensive.  If your
test were integer intensive -- like string matching, typing really fast
:) -- then Intel is likely to be better than Alpha, but AMD is likely
to be better than Intel.

-- 
