> sbnaran@localhost.localdomain (Siemel Naran) writes:
> Now how much work has Microsoft invested in order to make sure Linux
> and Windows binaries are compatible?  I would like to say "none", but
> that would be inaccurate.  The fact is that Microsoft has invested a
> *lot* of work to make sure that Windows is *incompatible* with
> whatever other platform available.

This comment strikes me as a little off-base.  The object file formats
of DOS/Windows were formulated a long time ago; Windows 3.1 was
out when Linux was a gleam in Torvald's eye.  As wonderful as Linux
may be, it did come along a little late in the game for precedence in file
formats.
While Alpha Unix and Alpha Linus at least have X-Windows and
reasonably similar library structures to ease interoperability, the DLL
structure of Windows is just too different from Unix to allow easy
translation.

Of what consequence is object file incompatibility for Windows?  MS products
may deserve your
contempt, but they can read an enormous number of file formats and effect
good translations.  MS placed RTF translation and PostScript translation
into
Word long ago.  Now third part utilities allow parsing of EPS into
Windows WMF.  There are good public domain versions of LaTeX and
GhostScript for Windows.





