Hmm, 
	I am not sure what is meant by "true" parity. I guess it means
the parity bit calculation is done in the memory itsself. 

This is not what is required but you will require 36-bit SIMMS, rather
than 32-bit. Oftern these are sold labelled as "parity", but in fact
the parity bit is generated on the Motherboard, by the memory
controllers.

Multias require these to be fitted in pairs. Some other Alphas, like
my PC64 requires them in fours.

Some old Compaq, IBM and Dell PC's ( About 6 years ago )required
36-bit SIMMS, or they don't work. Generally 36-bit stuff was reserved
for "server" stuff, the ordinary PC's made do without the parity.

36-bit SIMMS are quite expensive, 4 x 32MB cost me about GBP 250,
recently here in UK 

