David Binderman (dcb@pncl.co.uk) wrote:
: My Alpha 21164 CPU at 533MHz is 8-12 times faster than my 
: Pentium 133 MHz CPU.

[...]

: Even if I bought a 400MHz Pentium III, it would be about 400/133
: = 3 times faster than my Pentium 133 CPU. 

In all my (quite substantial) respect to alphas, this comparison is
simply bogus. The microarchitecture of the P-III is sufficiently
different from the classic Pentium to make linear scaling of the
performance invalid. (Even for identical microarchitecture linear
scaling over the 3x clock speed range is not very useful due to
the external interface and cache issues).

If you will bother to look at the actual performance statistics
(e.g. at the www.specbench.org), you will find that a 450MHz PIII
is a bit *faster* than 533MHz 21164 on SPECint, and is lagging by 
may be 40% on SPECfp:

   System    CPU                SPECint     SPECfp

   Various   533MHz 21164      16.6-16.9   21.7-21.9

   DS20      500MHz 21264      27.7        58.7
   AS8400    575MHz 21264      30.3        47.7

   Various   133MHz Pentium     3.9-4.0     2.6-3.5

   Various   450MHz PIII       18.6-18.7   13.3-13.7
   Various   550MHz PIII Xeon  23.6-24.4   16.8-17.1
            [400MHz PIII]        [16.5]     [11.8]
   (yes, I know it is against the rules to scale SPEC
    results. Still, I am not a system vendor, and you
    know that the "400MHz PIII" numbers above are bogus)

Unfortunately, SPEC database has no entries for 400MHz PIII-based
systems, so I simply scaled the 450MHz PIII results by 8/9th. If
this scaling is remotely like the truth, 400MHz PIII should roughly
on par with 533MHz 21164 on integer codes - which is all what will
matter for the examples you cited (compilation, program development).
Of course, a 500MHz 21264 easily wins over that Xeon on floating
point - but for integer work PIII still looks very decent.

Finally, if you will bother to go out and price *equivalently 
configured* 533MHz 21164 alpha and 400MHz PIII systems, I am afraid 
you'll find out that the prices are going to be roughly the same for
largish purchases (say a few dozen of systems). Depending on your 
circumstances, and kinds of discounts you'll be able to negotiate,
either alphas or PIIIs may end up being a bit cheaper - but the
keyword here is *a bit*. If you do not have the volume discount
lever, the PIII systems are increasingly likely to win on price.

In our case (see http://www.cobalt.chem.ucalgary.ca/cobalt/poster/),
FP performance was an overriding concern, and alphas were a big win.
Had we been more interested in integer performance, the answer might
have been quite different - this race is not as cleanly separated as
it used to be.

/Serge.P

--
