A plain PIII system will win in price, considering the fact that a
21164 will necessitate buying a very expensive motherboard. Expect to
pay around $700 to $800 for a 600MHz 21164 and approximately $700 to
$1200 for a motherboard. A PIII 550MHz will cost you about $700, and
you can get a motherboard for $100. Its performance will be about the
same on integer and noticably worse on floating point.

A PIII Xeon will give you faster integer than a 21164, but will
_still_ lag on floating point. You will still save on the price of the
motherboard, but you will end up paying $1000 to $2000 for the
processor---as far as I am concerned, a 21164 gives better value than
a PIII Xeon, unless running Intel-only applications is an issue.

It is a tribute to the quality of the Alpha architecture that a 3-year
old CPU (!!!) preforms as well as a top-of-the-line Intel
offering. And if you compare the 21164 to 3-year old Pentiums, you
will see that much more of a performance gap.

