	Contrary to popular delusion, the Multia does NOT parity check memory.
Parity checking is a very crude method of catching a small fraction of
the possible faults in a data transfer. 

	Parity checking works by setting one bit at the end of each binary word
according to whether the 1's are even numbered or odd numbered. 

	If an even number of bits are toggled, parity checking won't notice. 

	If the bits arrive in the wrong order, parity checking won't notice. 

	Even when parity checking catches an error, all it can do is cause your
system to halt. 

	The Multia *DOES* use the extra 4 bits on a 36 bit SIMM for ECC. That's
Error Checking and Correction. This uses the Reed-Solomon method of
ensuring that a data packet is correct. In a correct ECC implementation
if a packet arrives and fails the ECC check, it is requested to be
re-sent. 

	If you are missing those 4 bits, your Multia will not boot. Period. 

	A great deal of the SIMMS being sold as "Parity" are merely 32-bit
simms with some simple logic that calculates whether the bits in a
binary word are even or odd. 

	These simms will never, ever report a parity failure. They always
report correct parity regardless of corruption of data. Since parity is
a fairly worthless method of ensuring data integrity, these simms are a
reasonable solution to finding cheaper memory for systems that require
parity checking but are incapable of performing ECC. 

	If you install this sort of SIMM in a Multia, it will attempt to boot,
and if you're lucky you'll get to see a shower of ECC error messages in
the SRM console, and within seconds it will lock up. 

	Contrary to popular belief, ECC was not concieved to make up for faulty
memory devices. It was designed to prevent memory corruption due to
radiation. It can make up for small or intermittent defects in memory
storage devices, but it's primary design function was to ensure that
memory devices used in space would not fail due to cosmic rays. This is
a very real problem. 

	If you don't believe me, I have a friend who, when working at Raytheon,
helped design the serial communications bus used in the space shuttle.
He would be more than happy to lecture you for hours on the subject. 

	For more information on Reed-Solomon codes, see
http://com5.ee.uct.ac.za/courses/EEE350W/topics/rs/rs.html

	As an aside, it is extremely important that any SIMMs you install in
your Multia have gold contacts. If you install SIMMs with tin contacts
in a gold simm socket such as found in the Multia, electrolytic
differences in the two metals will cause both surfaces to corrode,
eventually rendering both the simm and the socket unusable. The same
will happen with a gold simm in a tin socket. 

	Any plumber can tell you why you shouldn't mix metals like that. Next
time you've got one under your sink, ask him (or her, should you be that
lucky).  

 - Eric

