Siemel Naran wrote:
> Suppose I compile a program on Alpha Digital UNIX.  So I have a
> Alpha Digital UNIX executable.
> 
> Can I run this on a Alpha Linux?  As it's the same chip, I feel
> that I should be able to do this, provided that I don't use any
> Digital UNIX specific dynamic libraries.  But my gut feeling is
> that it's not possosible.
Hi Siemel!

You can execute Alpha Digital UNIX binaries.
The problem is that the binaries do calls to the digital libs.
These call a not the same as in the glibc on linuxbased systems.
All you have to do, is to copy the original digital libs and the
libloader in your linuxsystem.
I have, for e.g., the netscape communicator 4.6 for osf4 running
on my box. All I had to do is to copy these files:
/etc/svc.conf
/sbin/loader
/shlib/libICE.so
/shlib/libSM.so
/shlib/libX11.so
/shlib/libXext.so
/shlib/libXm.so
/shlib/libXmu.so
/shlib/libXt.so
/shlib/libc.so
/shlib/libc_r.so
/shlib/libcxx.so
/shlib/libdnet_stub.so
/shlib/libexc.so
/shlib/libm.so
/shlib/libots.so
So you have to find out what libs your program need and to
copy them (strace is your friend ;).

The 2nd solution can be to link your binary staticly on DigitalUnix.

Regards,
	AleX



