I ordered the Red Hat 5.2 from www.cheapbytes.com, and installed it a
couple
of days ago.  It worked fine, although I had a little bit of trouble
because
I put my linux partition more than 1024 cylinders from the beginning of
the
disk, and milo (the OS loader) couldn't find it.  After getting a second
SCSI
disk, and putting Linux on it; it worked great.

In porting all of our code from an Intel Linux box, I find the
following:

1) Some things are twice as fast as on the Intel box; some are about the
same.
This is a little disappointing, but it makes sense.  The most important
application
that I wanted to run on the box was 2D compositing (for film effects),
and a
big-word RISC machine is just not that appropriate for 8-bit/channel
images; so
the compositing isn't any faster than the Intel box (even though the
Intel
box is 266 MHz and the Alpha is 533).  By customizing the code to do all
four
channels at once in a 64-bit word, I probably could double the speed of
the
Alpha.

2) Those afore-mentioned 64-bit words were a surprise.  There were a
number
of places in my code that I had expected 'long' to be 32 bits.  In
particular,
I call Mesa (nee' OpenGL) glDrawPixel commands with an array of
'unsigned long's
and was surprised that my pictures didn't show up correctly.  Converting
everything
to 'int' solved this problem.  Still, I'm sure that bugs still await
discovery.

3) There is no native Netscape for the box.  I know that Netscape is
just one
application among many, but it's an important one.  I'm going to try to
use
the em86 program, an emulator for Intel on Alpha; and see how well it
runs.

Anyway, that's my experience after three days of Linux/Alpha.  Enjoy the
adventure!

thad



