Author Leslie Donaldson

Announcement:
  Due to no current organized support for em86 I have decided
to take this project on. To be honest My main goals are to
be able to upgrade to redhat 6.0 and keep Netscape. I also
want to at least try to get wine and quake 2 to run.

Current Status
  I am close to being able to release a new version of em86 (A
couple of weeks) that will have NO new features. This will be 
known as a 2.2-0 release code name (Base release 1999)
  My code will currently only run Netscape.3.04 and Netscape.3.01.

Identified Programs
  I have identified the following programs to test against:
These work with 0.4-1
      Netscape.3.01
      Netscape.3.04
      Netscape.4.08
      Netscape.4.50
      acroread
These Don't work with 0.4-1
      quake
      quake 2
      quake 3 test

What I could use.
 - List of 386 programs people use with em86.
 - List of 386 programs people want to use with em86.
 - A way to have gcc link against older libc's.
     My Intel box is redhat 5.2 and I need to link against
     libc instead of glibc.
 - A way inside of C code or a make file to tell the 
   version numbers of the libc.
      /* glibc.2.0*/
      sscanf(&__libc_version, "%d.%d.%d", &maj, &min, &rev);
      /* glibc.2.1 */
      sprintf(version,"%s",gnu_get_libc_version());
      sscanf(&version, "%d.%d.%d", &maj, &min, &rev);
 - A replacement for this command.
      retval = __getdents(fd, tmpbuf, count);
 - People who want to volunteer to be my victims for a beta release.
 - Support from Compaq. I really need the source code to EM86. As a note
   I do NOT need to release the source code. I just need to compile it.

Future Plans
  - Support more libc applications
  - Support glibc applications
  - Support bochs cpu emulator again.
  - Make code ANSI compliant.

I will update my web site with more information sometime this weekend.

http://www.cs.rose-hulman.edu/~donaldlf

Please send all email to

mail://donaldlf@cs.rose-hulman.edu

Thank You
Leslie Donaldson

