In article <7hi4bh$1jp$1@dinkel.civ.utwente.nl>, "Sander Reiding" <s.s.n.sander@student.utwente.nl> wrote:
>Hi,
>
>I'm running a AXPpci33 board with RH6.0 installed (samba 2.0.3). I want to
>map a share in a tcp/ip network (windows), I need smbmount to do the job but
>this is not with RH included. I got a smbfs package for rh which include the
>source of smbmount and smbumount, etc but I cant make them because I first
>must do a kernel patch, this is also included but doesn't work. I use
>patch -p1 samba-etc.diff and nothing happens (prompt goes to next line and
>that's it must do a ^c to get prompt back. So can't compile smbmount ....
>who can help me out ?

If you are running Redhat 6.0 then you just need to recompile the kernel with
smbfs support.  The support is in the kernel already.  Then grab a copy of
the samba 2.0.3 tarball.  Read the included documentation.  It will tell you 
the ./configure flag for compiling in smbmount.  After compiling the package
you don't need to bother installing it.  Just find smbmount, smbmnt,  and
smbumount.  Put those in /usr/sbin or something like that.  The mounting
syntax is 
smbmount //server/sharename -c 'mount /mountpoint'


