I apologize for the long post and the many questions, but I would REALLY
appreciate any help I may get on any or all of these (and I have RT_M...)
:->  At the end of the post is a summary of a few thing I have figured out
that may be helpful to other Multia (UDB) users...

I am in the planning process for my first Linux installation, and the target
machine is a UDB/Multia (actually three of them).  I have read as much
material as I have found to lay my hands on, but need some real world hints
to get going in the right direction.

Q1: Firmware version?
I have upgraded to firmware 4.33 (which confuses me some, because I see all
sorts of references to 3.8(.2 & .3).  It seems to work (have already been
through a couple of slightly failed installation attempts).  Am I likely to
run into problems with it later?

Q2: Linux and upgrades?
I have a copy of Red Hat 5.1 (Alpha) on CD that I plan to install from
(RedHat PowerTools for Linux disk 2).  Will it be relatively easy to upgrade
to a newer version later, or is there a compelling reason to spring for 6.0
at this point (before I install to begin with)?

Q3: What disk partitions?
The documentation I have found about how to define my partitions (and which
ones) seem to point in all sorts of different directions.  I understand I
need at least 3 partitions - a "boot" partition with MILO and Linload, the
root partition, and a swap partition.  Some documents recommend a /usr and a
/home (and some a /var) partition.  I am installing to a 1 Gb SCSI HD, and
plan to add a second drive to it once it is up and running.  What partitions
should I create, and what sizes should I make them (considering that I will
add a second HD later)?

Q4: Large root or alternative approach to install?
One document said to create a small root partition (80 Mb) and a large /usr
for the software.  I am planning to use the machine(s) for learning Linux,
and for setting up one as a web server, so I initially chose to install
everything.  It appears that Red Hat wants to install it all to root, and of
course my root partition was not large enough.  Installing everything
appears to either imply about a 720Mb root partition, or a different
approach to installation.  How would a "seasoned" Linux user do it?

Q5: Disk Druid or fdisk?
One document (specific to the Multia and documenting installation of RH 5.0)
said that there were some problems with Disk Druid (that Linux didn't like
something it did).  Is that resolved in 5.1, or should I use fdisk (disk
druid seemed a lot more friendly)?

Q6: Disk Druid and Partitions?
Using Disk Druid, it would assign partitions 1, 5, 6, 7, etc.  Deleting the
partitions with fdisk after that would indicate 7 partitions ("delete
partition #(1-7)").  Attempting to delete partitions 2, 3, and 4 would
result in an error message ("empty partition" or something like that).  What
is the deal with this?  It almost appears as if any partitions added after
the first one are treated as extended partitions?  What should I do and why?

Q7: Dual (or triple) network adapters in Linux?
Will Linux support adding Ethernet adapters through the PCMCIA slots and
using them in parallel (different IP addresses) with the built-in Ethernet
(RJ-45) port?  Could I put a 100 Mbps Ethernet (PCMCIA) adapter in one?

Q8: SCSI rigging?
4:  I plan to try making an "extension" cable between the short, soldered-on
internal cable and the added external connector (bracket mounted - see
below) with a plug for an internal drive as well, so that I can connect an
internal AND an external SCSI drive.  Does anybody here know enough about
SCSI to tell me if this will be a workable solution?

Q8: Other?
Finally, is there anything else I should think about during my installation
planning?


Since I'm asking so much, I figured I should try to contribute a little as
well... :->

Some things I have found out:

1:  Ray-O-Vac 844 batteries (or equivalents) are good replacements for the
840 batteries.  The difference is that the 844 has a 1400 mAh charge (longer
battery life...) instead of only 800 mAh for the 840.  Radio Shack was going
to charge $20 for an 840 equivalent, but checking the local battery
specialty stores turned up a supplier with an 844 equivalent (in this case
same dimensions as the 840) for $6.25.  Well worth a few phone calls.

2:  The "internal only" SCSI can be converted to an external SCSI by way of
an internal to external SCSI bracket (the kind of bracket that is on the end
of all PC boards).  Get one at your local electronics parts specialty store
or from an online vendor such as CMP Express (www.cmpexpress.com).

3:  The 4 pins on the SCSI riser card are power pins.  I haven't found the
pinout (I guess I could use a voltmeter...) or a supplier for the power cord
to use it to power an internal SCSI HD.

4:  Creation of the (correct, workable) MILO disk with existing instructions
is a non-trivial affair... :-<


Thanks,
Tore.





