On Thu, 13 May 1999, Berth Ljunggren wrote:

[clip]
>9,1gb uw 10000rpm scsi disk, the graphic card will be a powerstorm
>4D10T or the diamond fire gl 1000.
[clip]
>Any comments?, recomendations for the graphic card?
>How will Linux run on this machine(fast i suppose),any problems to
>look out for..

I wonder if it is reasonable to purchase 3D-accelerated framebuffers for
your use - as far as I know it, there's no support for these cards
concerning 3D-acceleration in Linux (XFree86). I could imagine that your
rather powerful CPU with good quality and XFree86 supported 2D-accelerator
would be enough for your needs, if only lines, points and such ("there is
no filling") are needed when modeling.

Of course this kind of software rendering is not adequate (is this right
word to used here?) if lots of textures are involved. Performance would be
rasteration bounded, which is not where software rendering triumphs.

I've myself have bought old Hercules Stingray 128/3D for my rather old
Pentium 100 machine and it's been amazing how much it accelerates smooth
surfaces and textures in Mesa (compiled 3dfx support in) - I hardly can
believe it is the same machine. But when it comes to complex geometry the
Stingray is not of any help any longer.

Any ways, in my work I've used software rendering Mesa with 360MHz
UltraSparcIIi and there's no big difference to Elite3D m3 (rather advanced
OpenGL-accelerator) when the scene is not rather complex nor special
materials, textures or lighting are involved.

You could just buy "cheap" 2D-accelerator first and test if it is up to the
job - if not, you could consider buying 3D-accelerator which is supported
by commercial X-servers (and buy licence for this too).

Good luck with Alpha - I'd be interested to hear about your experiences
with machine of yours in the near future. I'm going to buy an Alpha too for
OpenGL-programming...

