Jacob Gladish wrote:
> 
> I was just reading an article about amd's k-7 chip, and they where
> talking about how it's compatible with alpha, blah, blah, blah...now
> what exatcly does "compatible" mean? Does this mean in a few months, I
> can go out and buy a new k-7 chip, and stick it in my as200?
> 
> jake

RE: the AS200 having a K7, no. . 

What this means, is that the K7 and the new 21264 are pin compatible
between each other.  Just like Intel created the Slot 1 and Slot 2
processors, AMD and the former Digital Semiconductor created the Slot B
processor.  Simply exchange the K7 CPU with a 21264 slot B cartridge,
reflash the firmware and away you go.  

However, this procedure took Alpha Processor, Inc. small amount of time
to make this promise reality.  This form of motherboard is running in
Alpha Processor, Inc's lab with a Slot B 21264 CPU WITH AGP!

Check API's webpage for details. . .

http://www.alpha-processor.com

-Bruce Faust



