Massimo Boninsegni wrote:

> for whatever reason it goes through the entire
> procedure without ever actually installing the kernel on the hard disk !
> After doing the X-Window configuration thingy, instead of asking me to
> insert the boot disk in the floppy drive, it simply says
> ``congratulations blahblah''.Sure enough, when I reboot the poor thing
> can't find the kernel...has anyone else noticed something like this ? I
> have installed RH on my computer (LX164 motherboard) many a time,
> usually with a few problems but never this one...

Massimo---
 The last attempt I did was redhat 5.9.7 (Starbuck), and I had the exact same
problem. The reason for it is that RH has not built that last step in, or it
craps out on the alpha... When I had that issue, I sent mail to bugzilla.
Curious that it hasnt been fixed in 6.0 either.
Regardless, here is the solution.
When the install is completed, and it asks you to setup a printer, do an <alt>
<F2> to get another login terminal. Then, insert the boot disk into your floppy
drive, and mount the floppy using the 'mount /dev/fd0 /mnt/floppy' This will
mount the floppy to your /mnt/floppy directory. (you may have to type
/sbin/mount because the dir paths may not already be set in there). Then copy
the kernel to the hard drive, by doing the 'cp' command. Now, go back to the
main screen <alt><F1> and finish up the install. When you reboot, it'll get the
kernel. (remember to put it under / .... or /boot)
Once you get the machine rebooted, recompile your kernel. When its booting,
you'll notice about 3 - 4 services fail. Don't worry. This is only because
you're using the stock kernel.
Hope that helps.... ask me if you need more detail.
Enjoy. :-D
