Optimizing Alpha Executables on Windows NT with Spike
Robert S. Cohn, David W. Goodwin, and P. Geoffrey Lowney

Abstract

Many Windows NT-based applications are large, call-intensive programs, with
loops that span multiple procedures and procedures that have complex control
flow and contain numerous basic blocks. Spike is a profile-directed
optimization system for Alpha executables that is designed to improve the
performance of these applications. The Spike Optimizer performs code layout
to improve instruction cache behavior and hot-cold optimization to reduce
the number of instructions executed on the frequent paths through the
program. The Spike Optimization Environment provides a complete system for
performing profile feedback by handling the tasks of collecting, managing,
and applying profile information. Spike speeds up program execution by as
much as 33 percent and is being used to optimize applications developed by
DIGITAL and other software vendors.

