Definitely odd.  I have another suggestion to add to the fray ... my
multia started rebooting itself, turning itself on and off, and so on:
it turned out that the power connector on the motherboard (P8/P9) was
oxidized slightly which led to electrical resistance which created a
lot of heat.  Well, the heat would cause further oxidation of the
connector and the cycle would worsen.

I took a screwdriver and scraped at the connector conductors until they
were shiny, and somehow scraped at the inside of the female connectors
coming from the power supply as well.  Everything is great now.

Oh, right, I also had to replace the battery -- I charged up a 3.8v
nickel-cadmium and plopped it in to replace the 4.5v alkaline -- it
works nicely.

