Sender: news@world.std.com (Mr Usenet Himself)
References: <3731A49C.AD1CB82@psc.edu>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Shannon Knows Compaq: BETTER ANSWERS to your questions about the "Q"
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.os.linux.alpha

Jacob Gladish wrote:
> 
> I was just reading an article about amd's k-7 chip, and they where
> talking about how it's compatible with alpha, blah, blah, blah...now
> what exatcly does "compatible" mean? Does this mean in a few months, I
> can go out and buy a new k-7 chip, and stick it in my as200?
> 

Not quite. AMD licensed the Alpha EV6 system bus for the K-7 processor.
As a result, K-7 and EV6 parts will be pin-compatible. Both will use
what AMD seems to be calling "Slot B." The result is that a common
motherboard can be used for EV6 and K-7 processors. The common
motherboard should reduce the cost of Alpha systems, since they no
longer will require a pricey, low-volume, Alpha-specific motherboard.

Terry Shannon
Consultant and Publisher, Shannon Knows Compaq
http://www.acersoft.com



